  "body": "Adds Qwen access through Alibaba Cloud Model Studio (Bailian), and gives the provider catalog the grouping metadata the Admin UI needs now that there are 35 providers.\n\n## Why four providers and not one\n\nAlibaba splits Qwen access along two axes at once, and **both are credential boundaries**:\n\n| Provider | Endpoint | Key |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `alibaba_coding` | `coding-intl.dashscope.aliyuncs.com/v1` | `sk-sp-...` subscription |\n| `alibaba_coding_cn` | `coding.dashscope.aliyuncs.com/v1` | `sk-sp-...` subscription |\n| `alibaba` | `dashscope-intl.aliyuncs.com/compatible-mode/v1` | pay-per-token |\n| `alibaba_cn` | `dashscope.aliyuncs.com/compatible-mode/v1` | pay-per-token |\n\nA Coding Plan key is rejected by the pay-per-token endpoints, and a key issued for Singapore is not valid in Beijing. Collapsing either pair would hand a request a credential its endpoint refuses — the most commonly reported Alibaba misconfiguration. Each provider also takes a base URL override, so the US region and workspace-scoped endpoints (`{WorkspaceId}.ap-southeast-1.maas.aliyuncs.com`) need no new code.\n\nAlibaba publishes an Anthropic-protocol path (`/apps/anthropic`) on the same coding hosts. FCC uses the OpenAI-compatible one because that is the dialect it speaks upstream, so no Anthropic passthrough is needed.\n\n## Reasoning: read, never requested\n\nThe profiles replay `reasoning_content` from the stream but do **not** send DashScope's `enable_thinking`, which some models on these endpoints reject outright — and the Coding Plan roster proxies third-party models (GLM, Kimi, MiniMax) that cannot be tested without a subscription. An unsent control costs thinking on some models; a wrongly-sent one fails every request. `extra_body` passes through, so a user who knows their model accepts it can send it themselves. Same narrow-rather-than-wide choice made for Cline.\n\n## Catalog grouping\n\n`ProviderDescriptor` gains `group`. The admin config response gains `provider_groups`, a `provider` id on every field in the providers section, and `group` on each `provider_status` entry. **Nothing consumes them yet** — the Admin UI lands separately.\n\nThe providers section currently emits **91 inputs into one flat grid**, so the association between a provider and its key, base URL, proxy and rotation fields is the structure that view has been missing.\n\n`group` defaults to empty rather than to a plausible value, so a provider that forgets to declare one fails a test instead of quietly filing itself somewhere wrong.\n\n## Verification\n\nAll four endpoints were probed live **before any code was written**:\n\n```\ncoding.dashscope.aliyuncs.com/v1/chat/completions        POST 401 {\"error\":{\"code\":\"invalid_api_key\",...}}\ncoding-intl.dashscope.aliyuncs.com/v1/chat/completions   POST 401 {\"error\":{\"code\":\"invalid_api_key\",...}}\ndashscope-intl.aliyuncs.com/compatible-mode/v1/models         401 OpenAI-shaped error body\ndashscope.aliyuncs.com/compatible-mode/v1/models              401 OpenAI-shaped error body\n```\n\nThat probe corrected two things the sources disagreed on: the international coding host appears only in third-party write-ups and **is** real; and the plausible-looking `token.dashscope.aliyuncs.com` hosts do not resolve at all (curl exit 6 — a command that fails is not a command that found nothing).\n\nAll four instantiate through the real factory with the expected base URLs, and an `ALIBABA_BASE_URL` override was confirmed to take effect:\n\n```\nalibaba              https://dashscope-intl.aliyuncs.com/compatible-mode/v1/\nalibaba_cn           https://dashscope.aliyuncs.com/compatible-mode/v1/\nalibaba_coding       https://coding-intl.dashscope.aliyuncs.com/v1/\nalibaba_coding_cn    https://coding.dashscope.aliyuncs.com/v1/\noverride honored:    https://myworkspace.ap-southeast-1.maas.aliyuncs.com/compatible-mode/v1/\n```\n\nThe catalog enforcement points caught the missing README rows and the frozen order exactly as intended. The new group guards were checked by breaking what they guard — removing one provider's `group` makes them fail by name, rather than merely being observed passing.\n\n```\nruff format / ruff check / ty: clean\npytest: 3311 passed, 6 failed\n```\n\nThose 6 are the documented PowerShell installer baseline and fail identically on unmodified `main` in the same shell. (The usual 8 websearch failures were neutralised with `env -u ENABLE_WEB_SERVER_TOOLS -u EXA_API_KEY`; GitHub runners have neither variable.)\n\n## Residual risks\n\n- **No live chat completion against any of the four.** No key is available for either plan or either region, so model listing, tool calling and the `reasoning_content` replay are verified against the published shape only. The first user with a key is the real test.\n- **The Coding Plan hosts 404 on `/models`**, so model discovery will not populate the picker for those two providers — same situation as Cline. The Token Plan endpoints do serve `/models` (401 without a key).\n- Example model IDs in the README and smoke config (`qwen3-coder-plus`) come from Alibaba's documented roster, not from a live catalog read.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
