    "message": "feat(providers): Alibaba Coding Plan and Token Plan, and group the catalog\n\nAdds Qwen access through Alibaba Cloud Model Studio (Bailian) as four\nproviders, and gives the provider catalog the grouping metadata the Admin\nUI needs now that there are 35 of them.\n\nWhy four and not one. Alibaba splits Qwen access along two axes at once,\nand both are credential boundaries:\n\n  alibaba_coding      coding-intl.dashscope.aliyuncs.com/v1        sk-sp- key\n  alibaba_coding_cn   coding.dashscope.aliyuncs.com/v1             sk-sp- key\n  alibaba             dashscope-intl.aliyuncs.com/compatible-mode/v1\n  alibaba_cn          dashscope.aliyuncs.com/compatible-mode/v1\n\nA Coding Plan subscription key is rejected by the pay-per-token endpoints,\nand a key issued for Singapore is not valid in Beijing. Collapsing either\npair would hand a request a credential its endpoint refuses, which is the\nmost commonly reported Alibaba misconfiguration. Each provider also takes a\nbase URL override so the US region and workspace-scoped endpoints\n(`{WorkspaceId}.ap-southeast-1.maas.aliyuncs.com`) need no new code.\n\nAlibaba publishes an Anthropic-protocol path (/apps/anthropic) on the same\ncoding hosts. FCC uses the OpenAI-compatible one because that is the dialect\nit speaks upstream; nothing here needs an Anthropic passthrough.\n\nReasoning is deliberately asymmetric: read, never requested. The profiles\nreplay `reasoning_content` from the stream, but do not send DashScope's\n`enable_thinking`, which some models on these endpoints reject outright --\nand the Coding Plan roster proxies third-party models (GLM, Kimi, MiniMax)\nwe cannot test without a subscription. An unsent control costs thinking on\nsome models; a wrongly-sent one fails every request. `extra_body` passes\nthrough, so a user who knows their model accepts it can send it themselves.\n\nCatalog grouping. `ProviderDescriptor` gains `group`, and the admin config\nresponse gains `provider_groups`, a `provider` id on every field in the\nproviders section, and `group` on each `provider_status` entry. Nothing\nconsumes them yet; the Admin UI lands separately. The providers section\ncurrently emits 91 inputs into one flat grid, so the association between a\nprovider and its key, base URL, proxy and rotation fields is the structure\nthat view has been missing. `group` defaults to empty rather than to a\nplausible value, so a provider that forgets to declare one fails a test\ninstead of quietly filing itself somewhere wrong.\n\nVerification. All four endpoints were probed live before any code was\nwritten: each answered an unauthenticated POST to /v1/chat/completions with\nHTTP 401 and an OpenAI-shaped error body, which is what separates \"wrong\nURL\" from \"dead service\". That probe also corrected two things the docs\ndisagreed on -- the international coding host appears only in third-party\nsources, and it is real; the invented `token.dashscope` hosts do not\nresolve at all (curl exit 6, not an empty result).\n\nAll four providers instantiate through the real factory with the expected\nbase URLs, and an ALIBABA_BASE_URL override was confirmed to take effect.\nThe two catalog enforcement points caught the missing README rows and the\nfrozen order exactly as intended. New guard tests were checked by breaking\nwhat they guard: removing one provider's group makes them fail by name.\n\n  ruff format / ruff check / ty: clean\n  pytest: 3311 passed, 6 failed -- the documented PowerShell installer\n  baseline, identical on unmodified main in the same shell\n\nNot done: no live chat completion against any of the four. No key is\navailable for either plan or either region, so model listing, tool calling\nand the `reasoning_content` replay are verified against the published shape\nonly. The first user with a key is the real test. The Coding Plan hosts also\n404 on /models, so model discovery will not populate the picker for those\ntwo -- same situation as Cline.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
